  "body": "## What changed\r\n\r\nA 4-axis Wire Contour had one widening for the whole cut: every ruling stood off the profiles as far as the worst twist needs. The straight stretches of a punch were then oversize. The wire verify also read a tessellated loft's facets as a gouge of a few microns, with no way to state the model's chord tolerance.\r\n\r\n- **Motion** (`crates/motion/src/wire_edm/ruled_widen.rs`): `WireWidening { PerRuling, Uniform }`. `PerRuling` is the default. Each ruling stands off by what its own twist needs. Transition rulings 1 mm from the ends of a long span end the widening near the twist. The existing ruled kerf law judges the emitted wire at every height. A path that fails it is refused by name.\r\n- **Sim** (`crates/sim/src/verify/wire/`): `WireJob.verify_tol`. The kerf law lets a reading enter the facets by no more than the stated tolerance. The threading law and the slug law do not use it. A tolerance that is negative, not finite or not below the kerf offset is refused as unreadable. A gouge names the deepest reading at any height.\r\n- **App**: the 4-axis block stores `widening` and `verify_tolerance` (0 to 0.25 mm at the operation door). The two values go through `EditOperationParams` and the project file. The widening is in the stamp digest. The post gate's report line states the tolerance and the deepest reading that it let pass. A 4-axis gouge with no stated tolerance adds a hint that names the model's chord tolerance.\r\n- **UI**: the operation page shows \"Stand off\" and \"Verify tolerance\" in the 4-axis block.\r\n- **Docs**: `site/content/guide/operations-edm.md`, `docs/decisions/1170-wire-ruling-kerf.md`.\r\n\r\nThere is no new command. The MCP surface takes the two keys through the operation parameters DTO (`four_axis.widening`, `four_axis.verify_tolerance`).\r\n\r\n## Readings\r\n\r\nMotion, square 10 to round 8, 64 points, twist tolerance 0.05 mm:\r\n\r\n- Per ruling: the middle of each side stands as a plain taper of its lean (less than 0.003 mm more than the kerf offset). The rulings next to the corners stand 0.015 to 0.05 mm more.\r\n- Uniform: every ruling stands 0.0207 mm more. The note says `the wire is held 0.1857 mm off both profiles`.\r\n- A punch with one twisted flank: `9 of 13 rulings stand within 0.0001 mm of the kerf offset`.\r\n\r\nApp, square 8 lofted toward a circle, faceted at a chord tolerance of 0.0117 mm:\r\n\r\n- No tolerance: `Punch1: wire gouge: the kerf of move 2 enters the part by 0.0004 mm near X-8.019 Y-0.833 at Z2.001 (64 move(s) gouge; the deepest reading at any height is 0.0112 mm) - a tessellated loft's facets stand past its ruled surface by up to the chord tolerance of the model: ...`\r\n- Tolerance 0.0028: refused, `..., past the stated verify tolerance 0.0028 mm (40 move(s) gouge; the deepest reading at any height is 0.0112 mm)`.\r\n- Tolerance 0.0117: posts. The report line says `kerf judged under the stated verify tolerance 0.0117 mm: the kerf enters the model's facets by at most 0.0112 mm, near X-7.468 Y-7.980 at Z6.000`.\r\n- Tolerance 0.2: refused, `the verify tolerance 0.2 mm is not zero or a positive number below the kerf offset 0.1650 mm`.\r\n\r\n## Pinning tests\r\n\r\n- `camcraft-motion` lib, `wire_edm::ruled_widen_tests`: `a_square_to_round_punch_is_widened_only_where_it_twists`, `only_the_twisted_flank_of_a_punch_is_widened`, `a_twisted_flank_ends_its_widening_at_the_transition_rulings`.\r\n- `camcraft-sim` lib, `verify::wire::tol_tests` (5 tests).\r\n- `camcraft-app` `wire_verify_tol`: `a_tessellated_loft_posts_under_its_chord_tolerance_and_is_refused_below_it`, `the_widening_and_the_tolerance_are_stored_and_bounded_at_the_door`.\r\n- `camcraft-ui` lib: `wire_four_axis_states_the_stand_off_choice_and_the_verify_tolerance`.\r\n\r\nQuick gate on the final head `fc6848cf0`, all green. Rerun on the final head: `camcraft-motion` lib `wire_edm` (33 passed), `camcraft-motion` test `wire_fans` (7 passed), `camcraft-ui` lib `wire` (15 passed, includes the pinning test). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-motion --all-targets -- -D warnings` and `cargo clippy -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings` are clean. Results after the merge of main, from the worker: `camcraft-app` binaries `wire_verify_tol` (2), `wire_4axis` (8), `solid_law_goldens` (7) passed. Earlier results on the same app code: `camcraft-sim` lib `verify::wire` (20), `wire_fans` (2), `wire_stamp` (7), `golden_cl` (33), `project_roundtrip` (73), `wire_taper_angle`, `wire_contour`, `wire_post`, `wire_tabs`, `wire_burnout`, `wire_readers`, `edm_conditions`, `conformance`, `mcp_wire_contour`, `sodick_wire_post`. The clippy run of `camcraft-sim` and `camcraft-app` was clean earlier and was not run again.\r\n\r\n## Screenshots\r\n\r\nThe operation page, Geometry tab, with the 4-axis block on. The images come from the test harness render. They are on the `screenshots` branch and are not part of this PR.\r\n\r\nBefore (main): the block ends with \"Twist tolerance\".\r\n\r\n![Before](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/blob/688073aeff311d345c75a109918bc3279d5cbb0f/pr-1921/before.png?raw=true)\r\n\r\nAfter (this PR): the block adds \"Stand off\" (default \"Only where the surface twists\") and \"Verify tolerance\" (default 0.0 mm).\r\n\r\n![After](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/blob/688073aeff311d345c75a109918bc3279d5cbb0f/pr-1921/after.png?raw=true)\r\n\r\n## Changed goldens\r\n\r\n`crates/app/tests/golden/wire_4axis_punch.cl` and `golden/nc/wire-iso/wire_4axis_punch.nc` are regenerated. The pose words moved by microns (for example `-8167` to `-8166`). The posted gate `posted_gates/wire_4axis.rs` now compares its direct regeneration within 3 counts: the per-ruling wire is one of several that hold the kerf law, and the one found depends on the vertices of the profile rings. The decision file gives the reason.\r\n\r\n## Not exercised\r\n\r\n- The posted program did not run on a real EDM control.\r\n- No fixture reaches the `tangled` refusal of the per-ruling path.\r\n- The batch tier and the release tier did not run.\r\n- No snapshot PNG changed.\r\n- An operation saved before this change has a stale stamp and must be generated again.\r\n\r\nChangelog: A 4-axis wire cut stands off the profiles only where the surface twists (uniform stays a choice), and it can state a verify tolerance for a lofted model's facets; a kerf gouge names the deepest reading at any height (#1170).\r\n\r\nEffort: max\r\n\r\nFixes #1170\r\n\r\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\r\n\r\n",
